Q: Reorder multiple ranges in Google Sheets with apps script? A: <h2>#1</h2>
<p>Try</p>
<pre><code>function reOrder() {
  const sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  [&quot;DY47:EA60&quot;, &quot;DY65:EA77&quot;, &quot;DY82:EA95&quot;, &quot;DY99:EA113&quot;].forEach(rng =&gt; {
    let values = sheet.getRange(rng).getValues()
    values = values.sort(function (a, b) {
      return a[2] - b[2];
    });
    sheet.getRange(rng).setValues(values)
  })
}
</code></pre>
<h2>#2</h2>
<p>or to keep formulas</p>
<pre><code>function reOrder() {
  const sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  [&quot;DY47:EA60&quot;, &quot;DY65:EA77&quot;, &quot;DY82:EA95&quot;, &quot;DY99:EA113&quot;].forEach(rng =&gt; {
    sheet.getRange(rng).sort([{ column: 131, ascending: true }]);
  })
}
</code></pre>
<h2>#3</h2>
<p>last solution, according to the complixity of your spreadsheet, in EC47</p>
<pre><code>=sort(arrayformula({DY47:DZ60,(EA47:EA60)*1}),3,1)
</code></pre>
<p>same for other ranges</p>
<p>you will need to show all rows</p>

Q: Apps script / Google Analytics API : combining multiple filters A: <p>I was able to solve this using the below snippet.</p>
<pre><code>&quot;dimensionFilterClauses&quot;: [
    {
        &quot;filters&quot;: [
            {
            &quot;dimensionName&quot;: &quot;ga:eventAction&quot;,
            &quot;operator&quot;: &quot;IN_LIST&quot;,
            &quot;expressions&quot;: ['value1','value2'] 
            }
        ]
    }
] 
</code></pre>

Q: How can I create a single function in Rust that accepts both sync fn and async fn? A: <p>You can convert <em>sync</em> function into <em>async</em> with <code>spawn_blocking</code> and add that</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>use std::io;

fn blocking_work(x: i32) -&gt; io::Result&lt;i32&gt; {
    Ok(x * 2)
}

async fn blocking_work_async(x: i32) -&gt; io::Result&lt;i32&gt; {
    tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || blocking_work(x))
        .await
        .map_err(|join_err| io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::Other, join_err))?
}
</code></pre>
<p>This should help if you have various existing functions, some async and some not, and want to consolidate them into one standard without refactoring their code.</p>
